 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <p>One or more questions for each outcome at the top of the page; Figures 1 and 2 cover outcome 1 and Figure 3 outcome 3.</p>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> You raise the nose for a climb. The attitude indicator shows the new attitude, but the vertical speed is still near zero. Do you pull more?</summary><p>No. The vertical speed indicator lags; the attitude indicator is the control instrument and already shows the change. Hold the attitude and let the performance instruments catch up before correcting again.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> On the panel in Figure 1, which instruments are control instruments?</summary><p>The attitude indicator and the power gauge. Airspeed, altimeter, vertical speed, turn and slip and heading are performance instruments.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> You keep drifting off altitude and are watching the altimeter more and more. What is wrong, and what is the fix?</summary><p>Fixation. The attitude is what is taking you off altitude, so go back to the hub-and-spoke crosscheck from the attitude indicator, set a small pitch correction, and let the altimeter catch up.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> Why trim only after setting the attitude?</summary><p>Trim relieves control pressure for an attitude you have already set; flying the aircraft with trim is not attitude control.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> In a long, level turn in cloud you look down at a chart. Suddenly you feel the aircraft rolling in another axis. What is it?</summary><p>The Coriolis illusion, from an abrupt head movement in a prolonged constant-rate turn. Go to the instruments and avoid sudden head movements in long turns.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> In a steady descending turn the instruments show altitude falling but you feel wings level. What will the disoriented pilot do?</summary><p>Pull back, tightening the spiral and losing more altitude: the graveyard spiral. Level the wings on the attitude indicator first.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> The attitude indicator shows a steep dive, but the airspeed and altimeter are steady. What do you do?</summary><p>Verify before recovering: the control and performance instruments disagree, so check the standby or other attitude indicator. If in doubt about the attitude indicator, use the attitude-indicator-inoperative procedure.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> Fixed wing, diving, inverted. When can you pull?</summary><p>Not until under 90° of bank: roll to wings level and upright while adjusting power or drag, then pull to level flight.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> Nose low, wings banked, airspeed building fast. What is the order of the recovery?</summary><p>Neutralize and analyze, power back and drag out, roll wings level, then pull to the horizon. Rolling and pulling together is a rolling pullout and can overstress the airframe.</p></details>

